INT. STAMP AUCTION HOUSE - VAULT ROOM - NIGHT

Glass cases of rare stamps under dim lamps. A long table, a velvet cloth, a pair of tweezers resting on a magnifier.

QUENTIN ASHBY-REYES, forties, in a tuxedo, studies a framed Inverted Jenny. NOVA, thirties, in an evening dress, stands beside him holding a champagne flute. CORNELIUS, fifties, the auctioneer, stands behind a podium counting a ledger.

He sets his glass down. She sips hers. Cornelius closes the ledger, takes off his glasses, and rubs the bridge of his nose.

CORNELIUS
The lot is sealed.

QUENTIN
Of course.

CORNELIUS
You're quite sure you want to bid?

QUENTIN
Sure.

Nova drifts to the case. She runs a finger along the glass. Quentin watches her hand. Cornelius watches Quentin.

NOVA
It's smaller than I thought.

CORNELIUS
They always are.

Quentin lifts the champagne bottle and refills all three glasses. His hand does not shake. Cornelius takes his without looking down. Nova takes hers and, in the same motion, tips it onto the carpet.

Cornelius frowns. She steps back. Quentin raises the bottle like a club. Cornelius reaches under the podium. Nobody speaks.

The lights go out.

When they come back, the case is empty, the podium is empty, and the three glasses lie upside down on the cloth in a perfect row.
